## Why two halves

### `byId` — the lookup

Reads and writes are a single hop, whatever the size of the collection:

```javascript
// Update — no scan, and only this one entry changes identity.
const byId = {
  ...state.users.byId,
  u2: { ...state.users.byId.u2, name: "Robert" },
};
```

The identity point matters as much as the complexity one. With the array, `map` hands every row a new object reference, so a memoised `<UserCard>` re-renders for all of them. With `byId`, `u1` and `u3` are the *same objects* they were before — their memo holds.

### `allIds` — the order

JavaScript object key order is technically specified (integer-like keys first, ascending; then string keys in insertion order), which is exactly the kind of rule you do not want a rendered list depending on. An id whose value happens to be `"42"` silently jumps to the front.

So order is explicit and lives in its own array:

```jsx
{state.users.allIds.map((id) => (
  <UserCard key={id} user={state.users.byId[id]} />
))}
```

## The payoff that decides the architecture

Reference other entities **by id, never by copy**:

```typescript
posts.byId['p9'] = { id: 'p9', title: 'Normalizing state', authorId: 'u2' };
```

The author's name exists in exactly one place. The post byline, the comment list, the `@mention` chip and the admin table all read `users.byId['u2']`. Rename him once and all four are correct — there is no second copy to miss.

This is the same argument as database normalization, and it is the reason the pattern is worth the boilerplate even when the collection is small.

## What the pattern costs you

The part interviews actually probe. Normalizing is not free, and saying so is most of the answer.

### Denormalizing to render needs a memoised selector

The component wants a post *with* its author. Rejoining them builds a new object, so an unmemoised selector returns a fresh reference on every render and defeats the whole point:

```javascript
// Bad — new object every call, so useSelector re-renders every time.
const selectPost = (s, id) => ({
  ...s.posts.byId[id],
  author: s.users.byId[s.posts.byId[id].authorId],
});

// Good — reselect caches the joined object until one of its inputs changes.
const selectPostWithAuthor = createSelector(
  [(s, id) => s.posts.byId[id], (s) => s.users.byId],
  (post, users) => ({ ...post, author: users[post.authorId] }),
);
```

### Delete leaves dangling ids

Removing `u2` from `byId` and `allIds` does **not** remove `authorId: 'u2'` from the posts that referenced him. You have to decide which, up front:

- **Cascade** — the delete reducer also sweeps the referencing collections. Correct, and expensive to write for every relationship.
- **Tolerate** — the join renders a placeholder (`Deleted user`) when the lookup misses. Usually the right answer for a UI, and it is what soft deletes exist for.

Silently rendering `undefined.name` is the third option, and it is how you find out you never picked one.

### Sorting is a derived view, not a re-sorted `byId`

`allIds` is canonical order — usually insertion or whatever the server returned. A sorted, filtered or paginated view is **another id array**, derived by a selector. Re-sorting `byId` does nothing, and mutating `allIds` in place throws away the order you would need to get back.

### When not to normalize

- A short list you always render whole and never address individually. Normalizing it is pure ceremony.
- **Data the server owns.** A query cache (React Query, RTK Query) already keys responses by request and handles staleness. Copying that into a normalized store means you now own invalidation, which was the thing the cache was for. See [Server state vs client state](/react-guide/27).
- Data with no stable id. Normalization is keyed on identity; if there isn't one, there is nothing to key on.

## `createEntityAdapter` — the same thing, renamed

Redux Toolkit ships the pattern so you rarely write it by hand. The only trap is the vocabulary: RTK calls the two halves **`entities`** and **`ids`**, not `byId` and `allIds`. Identical shape, different names, and interviewers use both.

```javascript
const usersAdapter = createEntityAdapter({
  // Optional. When set, `ids` is kept in this order rather than insertion order.
  sortComparer: (a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name),
});

const usersSlice = createSlice({
  name: "users",
  initialState: usersAdapter.getInitialState(), // { ids: [], entities: {} }
  reducers: {
    userAdded: usersAdapter.addOne,
    usersReceived: usersAdapter.setAll,
    userUpdated: usersAdapter.upsertOne,
    usersRemoved: usersAdapter.removeMany,
  },
});

// Memoised selectors over the normalized shape, for free.
const { selectAll, selectById } = usersAdapter.getSelectors((s) => s.users);
```

`getSelectors` is the piece worth naming out loud: it hands you the memoised `selectAll` that maps `ids` back over `entities`, which is the join you would otherwise have written with `createSelector` yourself.

## In an interview

The question is usually "how would you store the data for this feature?" and the full answer is three beats:

1. **Normalize it** — `byId` for identity, `allIds` for order, references by id.
2. **Name the cost** — a memoised selector to denormalize, and a decision about dangling references on delete.
3. **Name the exception** — server-owned data belongs in a query cache, not in your normalized store.

Getting to beat 3 unprompted is what separates "has used Redux" from "has designed state."

A [data table](/system-design/303) has a known number of rows. A log viewer does not — the data is unbounded, arriving live, and nobody will tell you when it stops. That one difference changes the API, the memory model, the scroll behaviour and the render path, which is why it is a separate design and a favourite prompt at any [observability](/system-design/111) company.

## Clarify first

| Question | Why it matters |
| --- | --- |
| **Live tail, historical query, or both?** | Both is the real answer and it is two data paths — a paged query backwards, a stream forwards. They meet in the middle and the seam is where bugs live. |
| **Volume?** | 100 lines/min is a `<ul>`. 10,000 lines/sec is this design. Ask, and design for the number they give you. |
| **Structured or plain text?** | Structured means expandable rows, field filters, and variable heights. Plain text means one height and a much simpler component. |
| **Retention in the UI?** | "Everything since the tab opened" is not a requirement anyone actually has; it is a memory leak someone forgot to bound. |

> "I'll assume a live tail at up to ~10k lines a second, structured JSON lines, and that the user can also scroll back into history. The interesting constraint is that the tab has to survive being left open overnight — so I want a hard ceiling on memory and a hard ceiling on renders, independently."

## The design

![A streaming log viewer — bounded memory, bounded renders](/diagrams/sysdesign/log-viewer.svg)

Three separate ceilings. Interviewers push on exactly this, because missing any one of them kills the tab — just at a different point.

| Ceiling | Enforced by | Without it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Renders per second | a `requestAnimationFrame` batch gate | main thread pegged at 10k renders/sec |
| Memory | a fixed-capacity ring buffer | heap grows until the tab is killed |
| DOM nodes | [virtualization](/react-guide/16) | layout cost grows with session length |

## The API

```typescript
type LogViewerProps = {
  // The component does not connect. It is handed lines.
  lines: LogLine[];            // the buffer, owned above
  capacity?: number;           // default 50_000 — the memory ceiling

  follow: boolean;             // controlled: the parent owns tail mode
  onFollowChange: (v: boolean) => void;

  filter?: LogFilter;          // level, source, free text
  highlight?: string;          // the search term, for marking

  onLoadOlder?: (before: Cursor) => Promise<LogLine[]>;
  renderLine?: (line: LogLine) => ReactNode;  // escape hatch

  emptyMessage?: ReactNode;
  errorMessage?: ReactNode;
};
```

**The component does not own the socket.** It renders what it is given and reports intent upward. That keeps reconnect, backfill and auth — see [real-time transports](/system-design/105) — in the data layer, and makes the component testable with a plain array.

**`follow` is controlled.** The parent needs it: a toolbar toggle, a keyboard shortcut and the scroll position all move it, and an uncontrolled version gives you two sources of truth immediately.

## The ring buffer

```javascript
// Fixed capacity, drop-oldest. Nothing here grows with uptime.
class LineBuffer {
  constructor(capacity = 50_000) {
    this.cap = capacity;
    this.items = [];
    this.dropped = 0; // surfaced in the UI — never drop silently
  }

  append(batch) {
    this.items.push(...batch);
    const excess = this.items.length - this.cap;
    if (excess > 0) {
      this.items.splice(0, excess);
      this.dropped += excess;
    }
  }
}
```

Two things to say about it:

- **Surface the eviction.** "Showing the last 50,000 lines — 2.1M dropped" is honest and useful. Silently dropping is how a user concludes the log is missing entries and stops trusting the tool.
- **A real ring buffer** (fixed array, head/tail indices) avoids the `splice` cost at high rates. Mention it; whether you implement it depends on the measured rate, and saying *that* is the senior answer.

## The frame gate

At 10,000 lines/sec, one state update per line is 10,000 renders per second against a 60fps budget. The transport is fine; the main thread is the bottleneck.

```javascript
let pending = [];
let scheduled = false;

function onLines(batch) {
  pending.push(...batch);
  if (scheduled) return;
  scheduled = true;
  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    buffer.append(pending);
    setVersion((v) => v + 1); // one render, whatever arrived
    pending = [];
    scheduled = false;
  });
}
```

At most one render per frame, regardless of arrival rate. Same pattern as the batching in [real-time transports](/system-design/105) — worth saying you have used it before, because it is the single highest-leverage line in this component.

If parsing is expensive (JSON per line at 10k/sec), move it to a **worker** and post decoded batches back. That is the natural follow-up and you should reach for it before they ask.

## Virtualization with variable heights

Fixed-height virtualization is the easy case and a log viewer is not it: lines wrap, structured entries expand, and a stack trace is forty rows tall.

- Maintain a **measured-height cache** keyed by line id, plus a prefix-sum offset index so `scrollTop → first visible index` stays O(log n).
- Estimate unmeasured rows, measure on mount with a `ResizeObserver`, and patch the index.
- **Expanding a row invalidates every offset below it.** Patch the prefix sums from that index forward rather than rebuilding.
- Keep an overscan of a few rows either side so a fast scroll does not flash empty.

See [virtualized lists](/system-design/306) for the general mechanism; the variable-height index is the part that is specific here.

## The follow gate

The behaviour that separates a real log viewer from a `<div>` that appends:

```javascript
// "At the bottom" needs a tolerance — exact equality never fires
// on fractional scroll heights and zoom levels.
const atBottom = scrollHeight - scrollTop - clientHeight < 8;

function onScroll() {
  if (follow && !atBottom) onFollowChange(false); // user took control
  if (!follow && atBottom) onFollowChange(true); // user came back
}
```

- **Following**: pin to the bottom on every append.
- **Frozen**: the viewport does not move at all. Lines keep buffering; the user is reading.
- The bridge is a **"↓ 412 new lines"** pill — it tells them they are behind, how far behind, and how to catch up, without moving anything.

Auto-scrolling while the user is scrolled up is the most common defect in this component. Say that sentence; it lands.

**Prepending history** has the mirror problem: loading older lines above the viewport must not shift what is on screen. Measure `scrollHeight` before the prepend, restore `scrollTop += delta` after, in a layout effect before paint.

## Search and filter

The design decision, not the implementation:

| | Client-side, over the buffer | Server-side query |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Latency | instant | a round trip |
| Scope | only the retained window | all of history |
| Live tail | keeps streaming, filtered | usually breaks the tail |

The honest answer is **both, and be explicit about which you are in**. Filtering the live buffer is instant and the user must be able to see it only covers what is retained; a historical search is a different query with a different result set. A UI that quietly switches between them is how someone concludes the tool is lying.

Highlighting: mark matches in the rendered slice only — highlighting 50,000 buffered lines does work for rows nobody will look at.

