## Edge cases to raise unprompted

- The stream disconnects — show it, keep the buffer, resume from a cursor not from "now".
- A single 2MB line (someone logged a base64 payload). Truncate with an expand, or one row blows the layout.
- ANSI colour codes in the payload — parse or strip, never inject as HTML.
- The tab is backgrounded for an hour: `requestAnimationFrame` stops firing, so lines accumulate in `pending` unbounded. Cap it, or switch to a timer while hidden.
- Text selection across a virtualized boundary — the rows you scrolled past no longer exist.
- Zero lines, and zero lines *after a filter* — different empty states with different messages.
- Copy / permalink: a stable line id and a deep link to a timestamp, so someone can share what they found.

## Common mistakes

- **Unbounded retention.** Works in the demo, dies overnight.
- **One render per line.** The transport is fine and the UI is frozen.
- **Auto-scroll with no follow gate.** The user cannot read anything.
- **Fixed-height virtualization.** Wrapped lines and expanded JSON misalign every offset.
- **Dropping lines silently.** The user stops trusting the tool, which is worse than a slow tool.
- **Filtering client-side while implying you searched everything.** You searched the buffer.
- **`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` for highlighting.** Logs are attacker-influenced input.

## Key takeaways

- Three ceilings, independently enforced: frame gate, ring buffer, virtualization.
- The component renders lines; it does not own the connection.
- Follow is a two-state gate with a hysteresis tolerance, and the "N new lines" pill is the bridge.
- Variable-height virtualization needs a measured-height cache plus a prefix-sum offset index.
- Say which data you searched — the buffer or the backend — and never blur them.
- Surface every eviction; silent data loss costs you the user's trust in the whole product.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"10,000 lines a second?"** — Coalesce to one render per frame, parse in a worker, ring-buffer the retention. The network was never the limit.
- **"The user scrolled up. What happens to new lines?"** — They buffer; the viewport is frozen; a pill counts them. Re-entering the bottom resumes follow.
- **"How do you keep memory flat overnight?"** — Fixed-capacity buffer with drop-oldest, and the dropped count shown in the UI.
- **"Variable row heights?"** — Measured cache plus prefix sums, patched from the changed index down, with an estimate for unmeasured rows.
- **"Search across all of history?"** — That is a server query, not a buffer filter, and the UI must say which one produced the results on screen.
- **"What if the tab is in the background?"** — `rAF` stops; pending grows. Cap the pending array or fall back to a throttled timer while `document.hidden`.

The [chart component](/system-design/311) question is "how do you draw a line". This one is "how do twelve charts share a time range without fetching twelve times" — and the answer is a boundary decision, not a rendering trick. It is also the most likely prompt at any monitoring or [observability](/system-design/111) company.

## Clarify first

| Question | Why it matters |
| --- | --- |
| **Who configures the dashboard?** | A fixed internal page and a user-editable one with drag-resize and saved layouts are different products. Ask before designing a layout engine nobody asked for. |
| **Live, or historical?** | Live means a refresh model and a "now" that moves. Historical means an absolute range and caching becomes trivial. |
| **How many widgets?** | 4 is easy. 30 on one screen is the fan-out problem this design exists for. |
| **Resolution and retention?** | 6 hours at 1s is 21,600 points per series. 30 days at 1s is 2.6M and the answer is server-side rollups, not a cleverer chart. |

> "I'll assume a live operational dashboard, up to ~20 widgets, a shared time range, and series that can be tens of thousands of points at full resolution. The two things I want to get right are who owns the time range, and making sure a 600-pixel chart never tries to draw 20,000 points."

## The boundary — where most answers go wrong

![A metrics dashboard — one range, one clock, twelve widgets](/diagrams/sysdesign/metrics-dashboard.svg)

The instinct is to make each widget self-sufficient: it knows its query, it owns its time range, it runs its own `setInterval`. It composes beautifully and it is wrong, for three reasons that all show up in production:

1. **Twelve unsynchronised timers.** Charts refresh at different instants, so at any moment they show slightly different windows and the dashboard subtly disagrees with itself. During an incident that is actively misleading.
2. **Twelve requests per tick** instead of one batched query.
3. **Zoom is impossible.** Brushing a spike on one chart should move all of them; if the range lives inside each widget there is nowhere to put that.

So hoist the shared things and leave the rest:

| Lives in the dashboard | Lives in the widget |
| --- | --- |
| `timeRange` (relative or absolute) | its query / series definition |
| the refresh clock — one timer | downsampling to its own width |
| the crosshair (hovered timestamp) | rendering and its axes |
| the brushed selection | its own loading / empty / error state |
| the layout | its legend, its units, its thresholds |

## The API

```typescript
type TimeRange =
  | { kind: "relative"; durationMs: number }   // "last 6h" — end follows now
  | { kind: "absolute"; from: number; to: number }; // pinned by a zoom

type DashboardProps = {
  widgets: WidgetConfig[];
  timeRange: TimeRange;
  onTimeRangeChange: (r: TimeRange) => void;   // controlled
  refreshMs?: number | null;                   // null = paused
};

type WidgetProps = {
  config: WidgetConfig;
  // Given, not fetched — the dashboard batches the queries.
  series: Series[] | undefined;
  status: "idle" | "loading" | "error";
  // Shared interaction state, passed down and reported up.
  crosshairTs: number | null;
  onCrosshairChange: (ts: number | null) => void;
  onBrush: (from: number, to: number) => void;
};
```

The interesting piece is `TimeRange` being a **discriminated union**. "Last 6 hours" is not `{from: now-6h, to: now}` — it is a relative range whose end moves on every tick. Flattening it to absolute numbers at the top is a bug that appears as "the dashboard stopped updating". Zooming converts relative → absolute, which is also what should pause the auto-follow, and saying that unprompted scores well.

## Downsampling — the number that makes the point

A 600-pixel-wide chart showing 6 hours at 1-second resolution is 21,600 points for 600 columns. Thirty-six points per pixel. Drawing them is work the user physically cannot see.

```javascript
// Bucket by pixel column, keep the extremes. NOT every nth point.
function downsample(points, width) {
  const bucketMs = (range.to - range.from) / width;
  const out = [];

  for (const bucket of groupBy(points, (p) => Math.floor(p.t / bucketMs))) {
    const min = minBy(bucket, "v");
    const max = maxBy(bucket, "v");
    // Emit in time order so the line doesn't zigzag backwards.
    out.push(...(min.t <= max.t ? [min, max] : [max, min]));
  }
  return out;
}
```

**Keep min and max per bucket, never every nth point.** Every-nth is the naive answer and it drops the one-second latency spike that is the entire reason someone opened the dashboard. The chart looks smooth and calm and is lying. If you say one thing about downsampling in the round, say this.

For production, name **Largest-Triangle-Three-Buckets (LTTB)** as the algorithm that preserves visual shape better; min/max is the one you can write on a whiteboard in ninety seconds and defend.

Better still, push it server-side: ask for `step=<range/width>` and let the time-series database roll up. Then the wire payload is 600 points, not 21,600, and you have fixed the network as well as the render.

## Render path

| Points on screen | Use | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| < ~1,000 | SVG | real DOM nodes, hover and a11y for free |
| 1,000 – 100,000 | Canvas | one element; hit-testing becomes your job |
| > 100,000 | WebGL, or downsample harder | almost always the latter is the right call |

Say the threshold and say you would measure it. "SVG until it stops being fast, then canvas" is the answer; a number with no measurement behind it is not.

## The shared clock, and the trace

```javascript
// ONE timer for the whole dashboard.
useEffect(() => {
  if (refreshMs == null || range.kind === "absolute") return; // zoomed = paused
  const id = setInterval(() => setTick((t) => t + 1), refreshMs);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, [refreshMs, range.kind]);

// One batched query for every widget, not one per widget.
const { data } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ["dash", widgetIds, resolveRange(range, tick), width],
  queryFn: () => fetchSeriesBatch(widgetIds, resolveRange(range, tick), width),
  // Keep the old chart on screen while the new one loads — no flicker.
  placeholderData: keepPreviousData,
});
